# DESCRIPTION

Taisce is VectraOS's one file system.
A volume lives on a disk's partition (sd(3)), and fs(4) serves it, as `/data` in the manifests that mount it; vx-disk(1) makes one, and vx-fsck(1) checks one.

A volume is one copy-on-write B+tree of 4 KiB blocks, its nodes 16 KiB: each file's and directory's inode, its directory entries, its attributes and its data's extents are keys in it, and nothing is changed in place.
Each node is checked against the checksum its parent keeps.
A node is a page, the same bytes on the disk as in memory: a header, an offset for each entry in key order, and the entries packed down from the page's end.
It is read and changed in place, never decoded; a page read from the disk is checked whole, every offset and length inside it, before it is used.
A volume records its format's version, 7; mounting takes 5, 6 or 7, and refuses any other (`unsupportedVersion`), and fs(4) doesn't format over it.
A version 5 or 6 volume becomes 7 at its first commit: a version 5 volume's blocks read as written before any snapshot.

Every change is made in memory, in a transaction group.
A group commits by writing what it changed, a barrier, its superblock to a ring of copies at the start of the volume and another at its end, and a barrier: mounting takes the newest superblock whose checksum holds, so a crash leaves the volume as the last group that completed, never part of one.
A file's sync makes what it wrote durable sooner, through an intent log, which mounting replays.
A failed write to the disk stops all writing: the volume refuses changes, and goes on serving reads, until it is mounted again.

Files and directories carry typed attributes, each a name and a value: a string (UTF-8, NFC), a signed or unsigned 64-bit integer, a double, a time, a boolean, bytes, a reference to another node, or a type.
Names are free, and by custom carry a namespace: `Audio:Year`, `MAIL:status`.

An index is declared on an attribute, with its type, and as caseless or not: from then on it is kept as files change, and what was there before is filled in.
A query (query(6)) is answered through an index; one no index serves is refused unless the caller asks for a scan.

The change journal records each change in order, a number each: the node, its parent, its name and why.
Watches (node(5)) and live queries are fed from it as changes are made in memory, so they may hear of one a crash then loses; a live query whose place the journal no longer holds is run again.

A snapshot keeps the volume as one commit left it: a record of that commit's root, named by the caller (fs(4) names it by its date), nothing copied.
Each node and each run of a file's blocks records the commit that wrote it.
A block the volume lets go is free at once if it was written after the newest snapshot; if that snapshot sees it, it goes on the deadlist instead, by the commit that let it go and the one that wrote it, and stays in use.
Deleting a snapshot frees those of the blocks the next snapshot kept that were written after the snapshot before it, which it alone saw; so no block carries a count, and deleting every snapshot gives back all they kept.
A snapshot's files are read as the live volume's are, through the same trees as they were then, checked against the same checksums.

A snapshot can be sent to another volume, whole or as its changes since an earlier one (send(1)): the leaves the two snapshots' trees share are skipped unread, each node whose keys differ is sent whole, but for its data, of which only the blocks whose checksums differ, and the receiver applies the stream into a directory as ordinary changes.
A stream is the files' plain bytes, whether either volume is encrypted: the receiver writes them under its own key.

A volume can be encrypted, made so when it is formatted and not afterwards.
Every block but the two superblock rings is AES-256 in XTS mode (IEEE 1619), each 4 KiB block a data unit whose tweak is its number, so a block copied elsewhere decrypts to noise.
Every checksum is then keyed BLAKE3 of the plain bytes, so the tree of checksums from the superblock down authenticates the volume: a block changed or moved without the key fails its parent's checksum.
Both keys are derived from the volume key, 32 random bytes made at format.
The superblocks stay plain, since they hold the keybag that opens the rest: up to four slots, each the volume key encrypted with ChaCha20 and tagged with keyed BLAKE3 over the rest of the slot, under a key-encryption key of one of two kinds.
A passphrase slot's is derived by PBKDF2-HMAC-SHA256 from a passphrase and the slot's own salt, 200,000 rounds.
A swarm slot's is derived by BLAKE3 from a secret bound to a swarm member's key by McCallum and Relyea's exchange over Edwards25519, as Tang's is: the slot holds the member's public key S, a point C = cB for a c forgotten once the secret cS is made, and the member's address.
To open it, a node sends the member X = C + eB for a random e, the member sends back sX, and the node's secret is sX - eS = cS; X is uniform, so the member learns nothing of C, and the slot's key never leaves the node.
The member's key is a multiple of 8, so an X with a small-order part gets the same answer as without it.
An encrypted volume's superblock also carries an authenticator, its bytes' checksum under the volume key: a copy anyone rewrote fails it, and mounting takes the newest copy that holds.
A slot is added or removed by a commit, which rewrites only the superblock.
Mounting an encrypted volume without its key fails with `locked`, and a plain one with a key, `invalid`.
AES runs in the CPU's instructions where every CPU has them, AES-NI on amd64 and the Armv8 cryptography extension on arm64, and otherwise, as on rv64, in constant-time software: four blocks at once, their S-boxes one boolean circuit over bit-planes, with no table indexed by a secret.

